course: adult-life
lesson: 1
title: A day at a grown-up's job
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Most grown-ups spend about a third of their weekdays at work, and a lot of what you hear at home is that work leaking in.
explain: At work there is usually a boss who decides what needs doing, colleagues who do it alongside them, meetings to agree plans, and emails that keep arriving even in the evening.
explain: Picture a kitchen in Leeds at seven o'clock: Mum is answering an email with one hand and stirring the pasta with the other, sighing about "another meeting that could have been an email", while Dad comes in late because the trains were cancelled again.
explain: None of that grumbling means your parents hate their jobs or are cross with you; it is how grown-ups let off steam after a long day.
explain: Knowing the words (boss, colleague, appraisal, short-staffed) lets you understand what is really going on when work comes home.
fact: A boss is the person whose job is to decide what work needs doing and check that it gets done; most bosses have bosses of their own.
fact: Grown-ups follow a boss's requests because that is part of the deal for being paid, and the boss's opinion affects pay rises and promotion.
fact: Colleagues are the people you work alongside, often for more hours a week than you see your own family; some become friends and some get on your nerves.
fact: Stories about annoying colleagues told at home are private and should not be repeated outside.
fact: A meeting is when people at work gather, in a room or on a video call, to talk through plans and decide things; many go on too long and leave less time for the real work.
fact: Phones let work follow people home, so messages arrive in the evening; many families make a no-phones-at-meals rule for that reason.
fact: Office politics is the unofficial side of work: who gets on with whom, who gets the credit and who gets invited to the important meetings.
fact: A business trip is travel paid for by the employer to meet customers or go to a conference; it is mostly meetings and hotel rooms, not a holiday.
fact: Commuting is the daily journey to and from work; many people live far from their jobs because homes near city jobs cost much more.
fact: Working from home means doing a job at home with a computer; the parent is still at work and needs quiet, even in slippers.
fact: An appraisal, or performance review, is a meeting where a boss talks through how someone has done over the year, a grown-up school report.
fact: An appraisal can affect pay rises and promotion, so people feel nervous before it even when they are good at their job.
fact: Being short-staffed means people have left or are off sick and nobody has replaced them, so the rest must cover their work too.
fact: When a parent seems frazzled after work, it is usually the workload, not anything the child did.
exercise: Mum on a call: "My boss wants it on her desk by Friday." Who is the boss? | The person who decides what work she must do and checks it | The person who owns the desk she works at | A friend from work who helps her out when she has too much to do | The person who pays the family's bills
exercise: Why do grown-ups usually do what their boss asks? | It is part of the deal for being paid, and it affects their pay | Because bosses can send workers to prison | Because bosses are always right about everything | Because bosses are usually their relatives
exercise: Dad at dinner: "Gary from accounts has done it again." Who is Gary most likely to be? | A colleague Dad works alongside | Dad's boss's boss, who runs the whole company | A customer who still owes Dad money | A neighbour who works at the bank
exercise: You heard a funny story about Dad's colleague Gary. What should you do with it? | Keep it to yourself, it was private | Tell your whole class about Gary at break time | Write Gary a note to warn him what Dad said | Ask Gary about it if you ever meet him
exercise: Mum sighs: "Another meeting that could have been an email." What does she mean? | The same thing could have been said in a short message | She wants all her meetings held by email | She missed the meeting because her email was broken | Somebody sent her an email instead of meeting her
exercise: Why do meetings make work pile up? | They eat into the time for the actual work | They make people forget what their job is meant to be | They are held at night so people sleep less | They cost the worker money to attend
exercise: Mum is on a video call at the kitchen table. What should you do? | Keep quiet and stay out of the camera unless it's urgent | Wave into the camera so all her colleagues can see you too | Ask her loudly what's for dinner | Turn up the telly so she can't be heard
exercise: Dad reads his phone at the dinner table: "Sorry, it's work." Why do grown-ups check work messages at home? | They worry about missing something important or looking lazy | The law says they must answer at once | Their phones only show work messages | They are bored of talking to the family
exercise: Mum says: "It's all politics at that place." What does she mean? | Who gets on with whom and who gets credit matters a lot there | She works in Parliament making the laws | Everybody there votes for the same party | They talk about the news all day
exercise: Mum packs a small suitcase: "I'm away on a business trip till Thursday." What is a business trip? | Travel her employer pays for, mostly meetings and hotels | A holiday her company gives her as a prize | A trip to buy a new business | A weekend away with her friends from work
exercise: Dad comes home late and cross: "The trains were cancelled again." What is the best thing to do? | Give him ten minutes before the big questions | Ask him right away to help with your maths homework | Tell him he should have left earlier | Ask him why he doesn't just walk
exercise: Why do many people have a long journey to work? | Homes near the big city jobs cost much more | The law says homes must be built far away from offices | Trains are faster the further they go | Bosses ask workers to live far away
exercise: Mum, at the kitchen table with a laptop: "I'm working from home today, so I'm not really here." What does she mean? | She is at work and needs to concentrate, even though she is home | She is ill and needs to stay in bed | She is on holiday but can't go out | She has lost her job and is looking for a new one on the laptop all day
exercise: "I've got my appraisal next week." What is an appraisal? | A meeting where the boss talks through how someone has done over the year | A test to see if someone can still drive to work | A party for people who have worked there a long time | A meeting where everyone decides together where the office will move to next year
exercise: Why are grown-ups nervous before an appraisal? | It can affect their pay rise and promotion | They might be sent back to school to do their exams again | They have to sing in front of everyone | They might have to pay a fine
exercise: "We're short-staffed, so I'm doing the work of two." What has happened? | People have left or are off sick and not been replaced | Everyone at work is shorter than usual | Two people are sharing one job so they can both finish early | The office is closing for a week
exercise: Mum seems frazzled after a short-staffed week. What is the most likely reason? | Her workload is too big | You did something wrong at school | She is secretly angry with Dad | She doesn't like her family
test: Overheard on the phone: "Yeah, she's my line manager, so it's her call, not mine." What does that tell you? | The other woman is her boss and gets to decide | The other woman works for her and must obey her | They are colleagues and both decide equally | The other woman is a customer phoning in
test: Your big sister says: "Gary must be Dad's enemy, he moans about him all the time." Put her right. | Gary is a colleague; moaning about him at home is just letting off steam | She is right, Dad should report Gary to the police | Gary must be Dad's boss, that's why he moans about him every single evening | Dad moans because he wishes Gary was his friend
test: Dad, eating: "bloody inbox, 40 emails since six, sorry kids." Why might the family make a rule about it? | So phones and work stay away from meals | So Dad has to close down his inbox for good | So the emails can be answered much faster | So the kids can read all of Dad's work emails too
test: Mum, grumpy: "Course Tom got the credit, he's mates with the director." What is she grumbling about? | Office politics, where being liked can count as much as the work | Tom stole money and the director let him off | The director made a mistake in a sum | Tom won a prize in a raffle at work
test: Your little brother says: "Mum's going on holiday without us to a hotel!" Put him right. | It is a business trip, mostly meetings, and she'll miss home | He is right, it's a secret holiday for grown-ups | She is moving to the hotel for good | The hotel is where her new job is
test: Dad walks in at 7.30, wet and muttering: "Two hours on that train, two hours." What is the kind thing to do? | Say hi and let him settle before asking for things | Remind him he promised to play football with you after school | Tell him the train is his own fault | Ask him straight away to sign a school form
test: Mum is in her slippers at the laptop and you want to show her a drawing. What is best? | Knock, then wait for her break | Shout from the stairs until she comes | Climb on her lap during her call | Leave it on the keyboard while she types
test: Overheard: "Got my review Thursday, bricking it tbh, if it goes badly no raise this year." What is she worried about? | Her appraisal might go badly and cost her a pay rise | She might break a brick wall at work | Her house is being inspected by a man from the council on Thursday | She will be reviewed in the newspaper
test: Mum on the phone: "Sarah's off, Jim's left, and they still won't hire anyone. I'm knackered." Why won't they hire? | Firms sometimes delay hiring to save money | There is nobody in the country who needs a job | Mum said she wanted to do all the work alone | It is against the law to hire anyone in winter
test: Your friend says: "If my mum's grumpy after work, it means I'm in trouble." Put her right. | It is usually the workload, not anything she did | She is right, grumpiness always means the child is in trouble | It means her mum wants to quit her job today | It means her mum doesn't like children
test: Dad: "Meeting ran over AGAIN, now I've got the actual work to do tonight." Why does he have to work tonight? | The long meeting ate the time for his real work | His boss wants him to do all his work at night from now on | Meetings always happen in the evening | He forgot to go to the meeting
exam: Identify the principal reason for which an employee customarily complies with the instructions of a line manager. | Compliance forms part of the terms of paid employment and bears upon remuneration | The line manager possesses the lawful authority to imprison a disobedient employee | Line managers are invariably correct in all matters | The employee is related by blood to the line manager
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the practice of commuting? | The daily journey between home and workplace, often long owing to housing costs | A holiday granted to employees in recognition of long service | The relocation of an office to a different municipality | A journey undertaken solely at the employer's expense in order to meet customers abroad
exam: Select the response that is consistent with the established facts concerning an annual appraisal. | A review by a superior of an employee's performance, bearing upon pay and promotion | An examination of the employee's dwelling by a council inspector | A celebratory gathering of long-serving employees | A medical examination required of every employee before any annual holiday may be taken
exam: It is given that an organisation is short-staffed. Ascertain the most probable consequence for the remaining employees. | They must perform the duties of absent colleagues in addition to their own | They are granted additional annual leave in compensation for the inconvenience | They are each assigned a smaller workload than before | They are required to work from home on all days
